1. Create logs.txt
Run these commands in the Cloudera Linux terminal ([cloudera@quickstart ~]$), not in Grunt.
cat > /home/cloudera/logs.txt <<'EOF'
google.com	10	100	2026-10-27
youtube.com	10	200	2026-10-27
google.com	11	150	2026-10-27
facebook.com	10	50	2026-10-28
youtube.com	11	300	2026-10-28
google.com	10	120	2026-10-28
facebook.com	12	80	2026-10-28
EOF


Verify the file:
cat /home/cloudera/logs.txt


Check the number of records:
wc -l /home/cloudera/logs.txt


Expected: 7
2. Upload logs.txt to HDFS
Run each command separately:
hadoop fs -mkdir -p /data/pig/simple_logs


hadoop fs -put -f /home/cloudera/logs.txt /data/pig/simple_logs/logs.txt


hadoop fs -ls /data/pig/simple_logs


Verify the uploaded contents:
hadoop fs -cat /data/pig/simple_logs/logs.txt

>pig

(i) Total views per hour per day
logs = LOAD '/data/pig/simple_logs/logs.txt' USING PigStorage('\t') AS (site:chararray, hour_of_day:int, page_views:int, data_date:chararray);


DUMP logs;


g1 = GROUP logs BY (data_date, hour_of_day);


r1 = FOREACH g1 GENERATE group.data_date, group.hour_of_day, SUM(logs.page_views) AS total_views;


DUMP r1;


(ii) Total views per day
g2 = GROUP logs BY data_date;


r2 = FOREACH g2 GENERATE group AS data_date, SUM(logs.page_views) AS total_views;


DUMP r2;


(iii) Total views for each hour across all days
g3 = GROUP logs BY hour_of_day;


r3 = FOREACH g3 GENERATE group AS hour_of_day, SUM(logs.page_views) AS total_views;


DUMP r3;

1. Pass a text file as input
If you want to pass a local text file as input, first upload it to HDFS from the Linux terminal:
hadoop fs -put -f /home/cloudera/Desktop/input.txt /data/pig/input.txt

2. Create the Word Count script in Pig Grunt
lines = LOAD '/data/pig/input.txt' USING TextLoader() AS (line:chararray);


words = FOREACH lines GENERATE FLATTEN(TOKENIZE(line)) AS word;


grouped = GROUP words BY word;


result = FOREACH grouped GENERATE group AS word, COUNT(words) AS count;


DUMP result;


You should see all seven records.
